※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=7029

※ Only a name stands in a template's braces: what is read out of a literal is one
※ word and never an expression, so that nothing the grammar does not see can do more
※ than name something.  A field goes in as an argument to a `{}`.  The refusal is
※ the macro's, at the invocation.
※
※ Compare: **Rust**, which refuses `format!("{p.x}")` the same way; **Python**'s
※ f-string, which reads any expression there.

let std := ⎕import("std")
type Point = x: u8 ; y: u8

@[startup, impure]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    let p: Point = Point(.x ← 1u8, .y ← 2u8)
    if std.format⌜"{p.x}"⌝ = "1": 0u6 else: 1u6
